---
import DocLayout from "../../components/docs/DocLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";
import ImagePlaceholder from "../../components/docs/ImagePlaceholder.astro";
---

<DocLayout
  title="Profiles"
  description="Learn how to use npkill profiles to clean up more than just node_modules - Python, Rust, Godot, and more."
>
  <h1>Profiles</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Profiles are one of the biggest features in npkill 1.0. They let you clean
    up much more than just <code>node_modules</code> — think Python virtual environments,
    Rust build artifacts, game engine caches, and more.
  </p>

  <h2>What are Profiles?</h2>
  <p>
    A profile is a collection of target directories that belong together.
    Instead of specifying individual targets, you can use a profile to search
    for all related directories at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, the <code>node</code> profile includes not just <code
      >node_modules</code
    >, but also <code>.next</code>, <code>.nx</code>, <code>.cache</code>, <code
      >.angular</code
    >, and other heavy directories commonly found in Node.js projects.
  </p>

  <h2>Using Profiles</h2>
  <p>Use the <code>-p</code> or <code>--profile</code> flag:</p>

  <CodeBlock code="npx npkill -p python" />

  <p>By default, npkill uses the <code>node</code> profile.</p>

  <h2>Available Profiles</h2>
  <p>
    npkill 1.0 ships with 17 built-in profiles. Run <code
      >npkill --profiles</code
    > to see all of them.
  </p>

  <h3>Web Development</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Profile</th>
        <th>Targets</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>node</code></td>
        <td
          ><code>node_modules</code>, <code>.next</code>, <code>.nx</code>, <code
            >.nuxt</code
          >, <code>.cache</code>, <code>.angular</code>, <code>.turbo</code>, <code
            >.parcel-cache</code
          ></td
        >
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>frontend</code></td>
        <td
          ><code>dist</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>.next</code>, <code
            >.nuxt</code
          >, <code>.output</code>, <code>.svelte-kit</code></td
        >
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Programming Languages</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Profile</th>
        <th>Targets</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>python</code></td>
        <td
          ><code>__pycache__</code>, <code>.venv</code>, <code>venv</code>, <code
            >.pytest_cache</code
          >, <code>.mypy_cache</code>, <code>.tox</code></td
        >
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>rust</code></td>
        <td><code>target</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>go</code></td>
        <td><code>vendor</code>, <code>bin</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>java</code></td>
        <td><code>target</code>, <code>.gradle</code>, <code>build</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>dotnet</code></td>
        <td><code>bin</code>, <code>obj</code>, <code>packages</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Mobile Development</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Profile</th>
        <th>Targets</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ios</code></td>
        <td><code>Pods</code>, <code>DerivedData</code>, <code>build</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>android</code></td>
        <td><code>.gradle</code>, <code>build</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>flutter</code></td>
        <td><code>build</code>, <code>.dart_tool</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Game Development</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Profile</th>
        <th>Targets</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>godot</code></td>
        <td><code>.godot</code>, <code>.import</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>unity</code></td>
        <td
          ><code>Library</code>, <code>Temp</code>, <code>Obj</code>, <code
            >Build</code
          ></td
        >
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>unreal</code></td>
        <td
          ><code>Binaries</code>, <code>Intermediate</code>, <code>Saved</code>, <code
            >DerivedDataCache</code
          ></td
        >
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <ImagePlaceholder
    alt="Profile result counter showing active profiles"
    description="The result counter shows which profiles are active and how many items were found for each target type."
  />

  <h2>Combining Profiles with Custom Targets</h2>
  <p>You can use a profile as a base and add additional targets:</p>

  <CodeBlock code='npx npkill -p node --targets ".tmp,coverage"' />

  <h2>Custom Profiles</h2>
  <p>
    You can create your own profiles in the <a href="/docs/npkillrc"
      >.npkillrc</a
    > configuration file:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`{
  "profiles": {
    "webdev": {
      "description": "Frontend web development artifacts",
      "targets": ["dist", ".next", ".nuxt", ".output"]
    },
    "mystack": {
      "description": "Full-stack project artifacts (JS/Python/Java)",
      "targets": ["venv", ".venv", "target", "__pycache__", ".gradle"]
    },
    "mobile": {
      "description": "Mobile platform build folders and caches",
      "targets": ["Pods", "build", "DerivedData", "gradle"]
    }
  }
}`}
    lang="json"
    filename="~/.npkillrc"
  />

  <p>Custom profiles will override built-in profiles with the same name.</p>

  <h2>Why Profiles Matter</h2>
  <p>
    Before npkill 1.0, you had to manually specify targets or run npkill
    multiple times for different project types. Profiles solve this by grouping
    related directories together, making it easy to clean up entire development
    stacks with a single command.
  </p>

  <div class="tip">
    <i class="bx bx-bulb"></i>
    <div>
      <strong>Pro tip:</strong> Set a default profile in your <code
        >.npkillrc</code
      >
      so npkill always uses your preferred configuration.
    </div>
  </div>
</DocLayout>

<style>
